    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nA sleep score summarizes signals such as movement and pulse using a manufacturer's algorithm. Different devices can give the same night different scores; the number does not directly measure how fully your brain has recovered. [1](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34314344/) [2](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/how-much-sleep) [3](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-apnea/symptoms)\n\n## What you can do\n\nFor the next week, record bedtime, waking time and daytime alertness alongside the score. Check your schedule, alcohol and device fit before changing training. Allow enough sleep for your age.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThere are no shared 25%–20% weights across brands. A score of 85 is not medical clearance for hard exercise; consumer sleep stages are estimates.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek assessment for persistent daytime sleepiness, loud snoring with breathing pauses or insomnia affecting daily life, even with a good score.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Schutte-Rodin et al. (2021) — Evaluating consumer and clinical sleep technologies: an American Academy of Sleep Medicine update](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34314344/) — Professional society review. Supports: Evaluation and validation limits of sleep technologies.\n\n2. [NHLBI — How Much Sleep Is Enough?](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/how-much-sleep) — Public health guidance. Supports: Age-related sleep needs; sleep debt.\n\n3. [NHLBI — Sleep Apnea Symptoms](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-apnea/symptoms) — Public health guidance. Supports: Snoring, breathing pauses and daytime sleepiness.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
