    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nSleep cycles through NREM and REM. Deep sleep is usually more prominent early in the night and REM later. Light sleep is a normal part of sleep architecture, rather than wasted time. [1](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/stages-of-sleep) [2](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34314344/) [3](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-apnea/symptoms)\n\n## What you can do\n\nPrioritize enough sleep and regular timing. Compare several nights on the same device alongside alertness. Shortening the morning to improve a deep-sleep percentage can remove useful sleep.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nStage proportions vary with age and across nights. A watch does not record a clinical sleep EEG; its percentages cannot establish a diagnosis.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nConsult a clinician if adequate sleep still leaves marked sleepiness, or someone notices breathing pauses.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHLBI — Sleep Phases and Stages](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/stages-of-sleep) — Public health guidance. Supports: NREM, REM and age-related variation.\n\n2. [Schutte-Rodin et al. (2021) — Evaluating consumer and clinical sleep technologies: an American Academy of Sleep Medicine update](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34314344/) — Professional society review. Supports: Evaluation and validation limits of sleep technologies.\n\n3. [NHLBI — Sleep Apnea Symptoms](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-apnea/symptoms) — Public health guidance. Supports: Snoring, breathing pauses and daytime sleepiness.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
