    "description": "Deep sleep changes with age and between nights.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nDeep sleep changes with age and between nights. A low watch estimate can reflect classification error, disrupted sleep or a changed routine; the number alone does not identify a cause. [1](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/stages-of-sleep) [2](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-deprivation/healthy-sleep-habits) [3](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-apnea/symptoms)\n\n## What you can do\n\nTrack total sleep, alertness and timing for one to two weeks. Keep the room dark, quiet and comfortable, limit late caffeine and avoid using alcohol as a sleep aid.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThere is no universal ideal deep-sleep percentage. A supplement, food or exact room temperature cannot be promised to increase it.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nLoud snoring, gasping or sleepiness while driving warrant assessment. Do not drive while sleepy.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHLBI — Sleep Phases and Stages](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/stages-of-sleep) — Public health guidance. Supports: NREM, REM and age-related variation.\n\n2. [NHLBI — Healthy Sleep Habits](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-deprivation/healthy-sleep-habits) — Public health guidance. Supports: Sleep habits and consistent schedules.\n\n3. [NHLBI — Sleep Apnea Symptoms](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-apnea/symptoms) — Public health guidance. Supports: Snoring, breathing pauses and daytime sleepiness.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
