    "description": "Sleep debt builds when sleep repeatedly falls short of need.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nSleep debt builds when sleep repeatedly falls short of need. Extra sleep can ease sleepiness, but a weekend does not guarantee reversal of all effects of chronic restriction. Large schedule shifts can also make returning to workday sleep difficult. [1](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/how-much-sleep) [2](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/sleep-wake-cycle) [3](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-deprivation/healthy-sleep-habits)\n\n## What you can do\n\nChoose a sustainable waking time and allow an earlier bedtime when short of sleep. If napping, avoid timing or duration that disrupts nighttime sleep. Reassess alertness after several nights with adequate opportunity.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThere is no exact hour-for-hour repayment formula. Catch-up sleep is neither useless nor guaranteed to erase every consequence.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPersistent sleepiness despite adequate opportunity warrants evaluation rather than simply more time in bed.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHLBI — How Much Sleep Is Enough?](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/how-much-sleep) — Public health guidance. Supports: Age-related sleep needs; sleep debt.\n\n2. [NHLBI — Your Sleep/Wake Cycle](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep/sleep-wake-cycle) — Public health guidance. Supports: Circadian timing, light and sleep pressure.\n\n3. [NHLBI — Healthy Sleep Habits](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-deprivation/healthy-sleep-habits) — Public health guidance. Supports: Sleep habits and consistent schedules.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
