    "description": "Pulse often falls during sleep, but brands calculate resting heart rate differently.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nPulse often falls during sleep, but brands calculate resting heart rate differently. Temperature, medication, illness, alcohol and activity can affect it. A curve alone cannot show exactly what your body is doing. [1](https://www.heart.org/en/health-topics/high-blood-pressure/the-facts-about-high-blood-pressure/all-about-heart-rate-pulse) [2](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29345524/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nTrack several nights with notes on changes. Check fit and measure your awake resting pulse if a reading seems unusual. Include how you feel when deciding whether to train.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThe commonly cited 60–100 bpm range refers to awake resting adults, not a required sleeping target. Training and medication can lower pulse.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nA sudden major pulse change with chest pain, breathlessness or fainting needs emergency care. Persistent unexplained change warrants assessment.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [American Heart Association — All About Heart Rate](https://www.heart.org/en/health-topics/high-blood-pressure/the-facts-about-high-blood-pressure/all-about-heart-rate-pulse) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Resting pulse, influencing factors and warning symptoms.\n\n2. [Kellmann et al. (2018) — Recovery and Performance in Sport: Consensus Statement](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29345524/) — Consensus statement. Supports: Individual recovery, training load and life demands.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
