    "description": "Slow breathing can alter HRV during practice and help some people relax.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nSlow breathing can alter HRV during practice and help some people relax. Comfortable pacing varies. Inhaling and exhaling for 5.5 seconds each is one option rather than a universal optimum. [1](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30245619/) [2](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27680542/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nSit comfortably, relax your shoulders and breathe gently a little slower for a few minutes. A four-second inhale and six-second exhale is an optional starting point. Return to natural breathing if uncomfortable.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nAn immediate HRV rise does not prove lasting health improvement. Avoid forceful deep breaths or chasing a score; studies use varied methods.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nStop for dizziness, tingling or breathlessness. New chest pain or breathing difficulty needs medical assessment.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Zaccaro et al. (2018) — How Breath-Control Can Change Your Life: A Systematic Review on Psycho-Physiological Correlates of Slow Breathing](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30245619/) — Systematic review. Supports: Slow breathing, HRV and heterogeneous evidence.\n\n2. [Draghici & Taylor (2016) — The physiological basis and measurement of heart rate variability in humans](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27680542/) — Physiology review. Supports: Mechanisms and interpretation limits of HRV.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
