    "description": "Easy walking or cycling can feel pleasant after exercise.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nEasy walking or cycling can feel pleasant after exercise. Faster lactate clearance does not necessarily reduce next-day soreness or speed muscle repair. Lactate is not a lingering toxin responsible for delayed soreness. [1](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29663142/) [2](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29345524/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nFor mild soreness, try comfortable movement and stop if pain increases. Complete rest can be appropriate with marked fatigue, poor sleep or illness. Prioritize sleep, adequate food and adjustment of the next session.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThere is no universally best recovery method. Evidence for active cool-down effects on soreness and later performance is limited.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nLocalized pain, swelling or unusual weakness warrant assessment. Do not push through a suspected injury.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Van Hooren & Peake (2018) — Do We Need a Cool-Down After Exercise?](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29663142/) — Narrative review. Supports: Limited evidence for active cool-down effects on soreness and recovery.\n\n2. [Kellmann et al. (2018) — Recovery and Performance in Sport: Consensus Statement](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29345524/) — Consensus statement. Supports: Individual recovery, training load and life demands.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "For mild soreness, try comfortable movement and stop if pain increases. Complete rest can be appropriate with marked fatigue, poor sleep or illness. Prioritize sleep, adequate food and adjustment of the next session.",
    "reading_time": "1 min",
    "thumbnail": "images/editorial/DM_RC_02_hero.svg",
    "source_name": "Van Hooren & Peake (2018) — Do We Need a Cool-Down After Exercise?",
