    "title": "Understanding Fitbit Active Zone Minutes",
    "description": "Fitbit Active Zone Minutes count activity using heart-rate intensity.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nFitbit Active Zone Minutes count activity using heart-rate intensity. A moderate-zone minute generally earns one AZM; vigorous or peak-zone minutes earn two. AZM can therefore exceed elapsed workout time. [1](https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/14236509?hl=en) [2](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity)\n\n## What you can do\n\nReview duration, perceived effort and activity type together. Beginners can build up from short sessions. Do not neglect strength work just because it earns fewer AZM.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nAZM is a product metric, not a complete medical measure of exercise benefit. Light walking and less sitting still matter without zone credit.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nHeart conditions or pulse-altering medicines may make automatic zones unsuitable. Ask about an appropriate exercise intensity.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Google Health Help — Track Active Zone Minutes or active minutes on your Fitbit device and Pixel Watch](https://support.google.com/googlehealth/answer/14236509?hl=en) — Manufacturer documentation. Supports: Fitbit AZM calculation; not a clinical efficacy study.\n\n2. [WHO — Physical activity](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) — Public health guideline summary. Supports: Activity recommendations by population.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
