    "description": "WHOOP Strain is a proprietary, non-linear 0–21 summary of cardiovascular and muscular load.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nWHOOP Strain is a proprietary, non-linear 0–21 summary of cardiovascular and muscular load. Moving from 10 to 14 does not represent the same effort as 0 to 4. Day Strain is not a simple sum of activity scores. [1](https://support.whoop.com/s/article/WHOOP-Strain) [2](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29345524/) [3](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23247672/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nCompare similar days within your own history. Add perceived effort, soreness and actual performance. Modify training for fatigue or pain even when the app suggests more load.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nManufacturer documentation explains the product, not a guarantee of injury prevention or muscle growth. Do not convert points into calories, cortisol or tissue damage.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPersistent fatigue and falling performance warrant review of health, nutrition and training with a qualified professional.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [WHOOP — WHOOP Strain](https://support.whoop.com/s/article/WHOOP-Strain) — Manufacturer documentation. Supports: WHOOP-specific non-linear 0–21 load score.\n\n2. [Kellmann et al. (2018) — Recovery and Performance in Sport: Consensus Statement](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29345524/) — Consensus statement. Supports: Individual recovery, training load and life demands.\n\n3. [Meeusen et al. (2013) — Prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of the overtraining syndrome](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23247672/) — Joint consensus statement. Supports: Overtraining, exclusion diagnosis and absence of a single marker.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
