    "title": "Zone 2 cardio: a practical starting point",
    "description": "Zone 2 means different things in different zone systems.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nZone 2 means different things in different zone systems. The talk test offers a simple estimate of moderate effort: conversation is possible but singing is difficult. It is not a laboratory threshold measurement. [1](https://www.cdc.gov/physicalactivity/basics/measuring/index.html) [2](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity)\n\n## What you can do\n\nChoose sustainable walking, cycling or another enjoyable activity. Start with a manageable duration, build gradually and include strength work and recovery during the week.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nAge-predicted pulse formulas are rough estimates. Zone 2 is neither the only beneficial intensity nor a guarantee of greater long-term fat loss.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nStop for chest pain, faintness or unusual breathlessness. Account for medical conditions and medicines in your plan.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [CDC — Measuring Physical Activity Intensity](https://www.cdc.gov/physicalactivity/basics/measuring/index.html) — Public health guidance. Supports: Talk test for moderate and vigorous activity.\n\n2. [WHO — Physical activity](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) — Public health guideline summary. Supports: Activity recommendations by population.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Choose sustainable walking, cycling or another enjoyable activity. Start with a manageable duration, build gradually and include strength work and recovery during the week.",
    "reading_time": "1 min",
    "thumbnail": "images/editorial/DM_AC_01_hero.svg",
    "source_name": "CDC — Measuring Physical Activity Intensity",
    "source_url": "https://www.cdc.gov/physicalactivity/basics/measuring/index.html",
