    "trigger": "User navigates to the Activity Screen or Discover tab",
    "title": "Training fatigue and persistent underperformance",
    "description": "Fatigue after a hard session differs from a sustained drop in performance.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nFatigue after a hard session differs from a sustained drop in performance. Overtraining syndrome requires professional evaluation and exclusion of illness, low energy availability, anaemia and other causes. No single HRV reading or laboratory marker establishes it. [1](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23247672/) [2](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29345524/) [3](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073)\n\n## What you can do\n\nWhen fatigue accumulates, reduce training stress and record sleep, food intake, mood and performance. Resume progressively as these improve. Use a flexible plan rather than chasing device targets.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nA fixed deload duration is not a guaranteed treatment. HRV can move in either direction for different reasons and cannot identify the cause alone.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek assessment for persistent underperformance, unintended weight loss, missed periods or repeated injuries.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Meeusen et al. (2013) — Prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of the overtraining syndrome](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23247672/) — Joint consensus statement. Supports: Overtraining, exclusion diagnosis and absence of a single marker.\n\n2. [Kellmann et al. (2018) — Recovery and Performance in Sport: Consensus Statement](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29345524/) — Consensus statement. Supports: Individual recovery, training load and life demands.\n\n3. [Mountjoy et al. (2023) — IOC consensus statement on Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (REDs)](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073) — Consensus statement. Supports: Low energy availability, bone, menstrual health and multidisciplinary assessment.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "When fatigue accumulates, reduce training stress and record sleep, food intake, mood and performance. Resume progressively as these improve. Use a flexible plan rather than chasing device targets.",
