    "description": "VO2 max describes maximal oxygen use during exertion.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nVO2 max describes maximal oxygen use during exertion. Cardiorespiratory fitness is an important health marker, but a watch estimates it using an algorithm rather than measuring gas exchange during an exercise test. [1](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27881567/) [2](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity)\n\n## What you can do\n\nCompare trends over weeks under similar activity conditions. Build regular activity gradually and notice practical changes such as easier walking or stair climbing.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nNo VO2 max value predicts an individual's remaining lifespan. Algorithms, temperature, entered weight and pulse data can affect estimates.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nNew breathlessness or a marked loss of exercise capacity warrants assessment even with a reassuring watch estimate.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Ross et al. (2016) — Importance of Assessing Cardiorespiratory Fitness in Clinical Practice](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27881567/) — AHA scientific statement. Supports: Cardiorespiratory fitness and health risk assessment.\n\n2. [WHO — Physical activity](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) — Public health guideline summary. Supports: Activity recommendations by population.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
