    "title": "Physiological sighing: evidence and comfortable practice",
    "description": "A remote trial compared brief daily breathing practices with meditation and reported mood benefits.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nA remote trial compared brief daily breathing practices with meditation and reported mood benefits. One practice used a double inhale and extended exhale. This does not show that a minute of sighing treats stress or lowers everyone's pulse. [1](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36630953/) [2](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30245619/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nIf trying it, sit comfortably, inhale gently, add a small second inhale and exhale slowly without straining. Use a few comfortable cycles and return to normal breathing rather than repeatedly taking maximal breaths.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThe study used roughly five minutes daily for one month. Its findings do not establish instant effects or replace mental-health treatment.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nStop if light-headed or uncomfortable. New breathing or cardiac symptoms require assessment.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Balban et al. (2023) — Brief structured respiration practices enhance mood and reduce physiological arousal](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36630953/) — Randomized remote trial. Supports: Five-minute daily breathing over one month; not evidence of an instant cure.\n\n2. [Zaccaro et al. (2018) — How Breath-Control Can Change Your Life: A Systematic Review on Psycho-Physiological Correlates of Slow Breathing](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30245619/) — Systematic review. Supports: Slow breathing, HRV and heterogeneous evidence.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
