    "description": "A cycle runs from the first day of one period to the first day of the next.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nA cycle runs from the first day of one period to the first day of the next. Its phases reflect ovarian and uterine changes, but cycles are not invariably 28 days and ovulation is not fixed to day 14. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/) [2](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/fertility-awareness-based-methods-of-family-planning) [3](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nRecord start dates, duration, bleeding and symptoms in a sustainable way. Use the record to prepare for symptoms and medical discussions rather than to dictate daily capability.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nEnergy and mood patterns differ between people. Hormonal medication, postpartum changes and perimenopause can make calendar phase predictions less reliable.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nBleeding between periods, after sex or after menopause, or repeatedly missed periods, warrants assessment.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Periods](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Cycles, symptoms and abnormal bleeding.\n\n2. [ACOG — Fertility Awareness-Based Methods of Family Planning](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/fertility-awareness-based-methods-of-family-planning) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Mucus, BBT, fertile timing and contraceptive limitations.\n\n3. [NHS — Missed or late periods](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Causes of missed periods and when to seek care.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Record start dates, duration, bleeding and symptoms in a sustainable way. Use the record to prepare for symptoms and medical discussions rather than to dictate daily capability.",
