    "title": "Tracking cervical mucus usefully",
    "description": "Mucus often becomes clearer and more slippery near fertile days.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nMucus often becomes clearer and more slippery near fertile days. Semen, lubricants, medication and infection can complicate interpretation. One observation cannot confirm an ovulation date. [1](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/fertility-awareness-based-methods-of-family-planning) [2](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/vulvovaginal-health)\n\n## What you can do\n\nUse consistent daily descriptions rather than inferring hormone concentrations. Combine signs and advice when trying to conceive. For contraception, learn a defined method and understand its real-world effectiveness.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nA mucus-tracking app is not automatically validated contraception. Appearance alone also cannot diagnose an infection.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek care for new odor, itching, pain, bleeding or pelvic discomfort rather than choosing treatment from a color chart.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [ACOG — Fertility Awareness-Based Methods of Family Planning](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/fertility-awareness-based-methods-of-family-planning) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Mucus, BBT, fertile timing and contraceptive limitations.\n\n2. [ACOG — Vulvovaginal Health](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/vulvovaginal-health) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Vulvovaginal care, discharge, tissue changes and treatments.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
