    "title": "Cycle-aware training without rigid phase rules",
    "description": "Systematic reviews find generally small average phase-related performance effects with limited evidence.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nSystematic reviews find generally small average phase-related performance effects with limited evidence. There is no strong basis for prescribing the same heavy and light training phases to everyone. [1](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32661839/) [2](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37033884/) [3](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073)\n\n## What you can do\n\nKeep a sustainable plan and note pain, energy and effort. If symptoms repeatedly follow a personal pattern over several cycles, adjust sessions or reduce load based on what you experience.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nSymptom tracking does not measure estrogen or progesterone. Findings in natural cycles do not automatically apply to hormonal contraception.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSevere pain, missed periods or sustained underperformance warrant assessment rather than being accepted as inevitable.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [McNulty et al. (2020) — The Effects of Menstrual Cycle Phase on Exercise Performance in Eumenorrheic Women](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32661839/) — Systematic review and meta-analysis. Supports: Small average effects and low-quality evidence; individualized approach.\n\n2. [Colenso-Semple et al. (2023) — Current evidence shows no influence of women's menstrual cycle phase on acute strength performance or adaptations to resistance exercise training](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37033884/) — Umbrella review. Supports: Insufficient basis for fixed phase-based resistance programming.\n\n3. [Mountjoy et al. (2023) — IOC consensus statement on Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (REDs)](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073) — Consensus statement. Supports: Low energy availability, bone, menstrual health and multidisciplinary assessment.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
