    "description": "Conception is most likely in the days before and around ovulation because sperm can survive for several days while the egg has a shorter lifespan.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nConception is most likely in the days before and around ovulation because sperm can survive for several days while the egg has a shorter lifespan. Ovulation timing varies even with fairly regular periods. [1](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/fertility-awareness-based-methods-of-family-planning) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nWhen trying to conceive, regular intercourse during likely fertile days can be more practical than targeting one exact hour. Discuss mucus observations or LH testing with a professional if needed.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nA roughly six-day biological window is not a safe contraceptive calendar. An app or wearable cannot be assumed to identify every potentially fertile day.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek earlier advice for very irregular cycles or known fertility-affecting conditions rather than relying solely on app timing.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [ACOG — Fertility Awareness-Based Methods of Family Planning](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/fertility-awareness-based-methods-of-family-planning) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Mucus, BBT, fertile timing and contraceptive limitations.\n\n2. [NHS — Missed or late periods](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Causes of missed periods and when to seek care.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
