    "description": "PMS can include mood changes, sleep difficulties, breast tenderness and appetite changes before a period.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nPMS can include mood changes, sleep difficulties, breast tenderness and appetite changes before a period. Cramps may overlap, but not all pelvic pain is PMS. Symptoms and their impact vary. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/) [3](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet)\n\n## What you can do\n\nKeep a symptom diary for at least two cycles, including onset, resolution and daily-life impact. Maintain balanced meals, comfortable activity and adequate sleep, then use the diary to discuss treatment options.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThere is no mandatory phase-specific diet or justification for high-dose supplements from cravings alone. Appetite changes do not diagnose a nutrient deficiency.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek care when pain or mood symptoms impair daily life, extend beyond the premenstrual period or do not improve with self-care.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — PMS (premenstrual syndrome)](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/) — Public health guidance. Supports: PMS symptoms, symptom diaries and care options.\n\n2. [NHS — Periods](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Cycles, symptoms and abnormal bleeding.\n\n3. [WHO — Healthy diet](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet) — Public health guidance. Supports: Dietary variety and food quality.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
