    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nThe pelvic floor supports pelvic organs and contributes to urinary control and sexual function. Good function requires both contraction and relaxation. Pain or dysfunction does not always mean weakness. [1](https://www.nth.nhs.uk/resources/pelvic-floor-exercises-for-men/) [2](https://www.royalberkshire.nhs.uk/media/avwg3hls/pelvic-floor-strengthening-and-relaxation-exercises-for-men_jun24.pdf) [3](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes)\n\n## What you can do\n\nTo identify the muscles, gently imagine stopping wind and lifting the pelvic floor while breathing normally. Relax fully afterward. A pelvic-health physiotherapist can check uncertain technique.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nPelvic muscles do not determine erection quality on their own. Do not routinely train by interrupting urination or tightening maximally all day.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPelvic pain, difficulty urinating or worsening symptoms with contraction warrant assessment before increasing exercise.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [North Tees and Hartlepool NHS — Pelvic Floor Exercises for Men](https://www.nth.nhs.uk/resources/pelvic-floor-exercises-for-men/) — Physiotherapy patient guidance. Supports: Identifying, contracting and relaxing male pelvic floor muscles.\n\n2. [Royal Berkshire NHS (2024) — Pelvic floor strengthening and relaxation exercises for men](https://www.royalberkshire.nhs.uk/media/avwg3hls/pelvic-floor-strengthening-and-relaxation-exercises-for-men_jun24.pdf) — Physiotherapy patient guidance. Supports: Strengthening and relaxation; not a prescription to tighten for everyone.\n\n3. [NIDDK — Symptoms & Causes of Erectile Dysfunction](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes) — Public health guidance. Supports: Vascular, neurological, medication and psychological causes of ED.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
