    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nPelvic-floor training is used for selected functional problems, but results depend on the cause and guidance. Erection and ejaculation difficulties are multifactorial; an app program is not equivalent to individualized treatment. [1](https://www.royalberkshire.nhs.uk/media/avwg3hls/pelvic-floor-strengthening-and-relaxation-exercises-for-men_jun24.pdf) [2](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/treatment) [3](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/ejaculation-problems/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nClarify the main problem, onset, medicines and associated symptoms. If considering pelvic rehabilitation, discuss realistic goals and monitoring alongside a medical assessment of possible causes.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nDo not infer guaranteed cure rates or timelines from small studies. More contractions are not necessarily helpful when muscles are already tense.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPersistent difficulties, pain, urinary problems or sudden changes warrant assessment rather than waiting to finish an exercise course.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Royal Berkshire NHS (2024) — Pelvic floor strengthening and relaxation exercises for men](https://www.royalberkshire.nhs.uk/media/avwg3hls/pelvic-floor-strengthening-and-relaxation-exercises-for-men_jun24.pdf) — Physiotherapy patient guidance. Supports: Strengthening and relaxation; not a prescription to tighten for everyone.\n\n2. [NIDDK — Treatment for Erectile Dysfunction](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/treatment) — Public health guidance. Supports: Cause-based care, lifestyle changes and professional treatment.\n\n3. [NHS — Ejaculation problems](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/ejaculation-problems/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Ejaculatory difficulties and treatment options.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
