    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nGentle movement can feel comfortable, but groin and pelvic pain can arise from muscles, joints or nerves. A stretch does not demonstrate release of the pudendal nerve or restored arterial flow. [1](https://www.royalberkshire.nhs.uk/media/avwg3hls/pelvic-floor-strengthening-and-relaxation-exercises-for-men_jun24.pdf) [2](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes) [3](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/testicle-pain/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nUse pain-free movement, breathe normally and stop for numbness, tingling or radiating pain. Note timing and sitting positions if symptoms relate to prolonged sitting.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nDo not force stretches or treat mobility exercises as an erectile-dysfunction cure. Pelvic muscle overactivity may require an individualized plan.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPersistent pain, perineal numbness or urinary changes need assessment. Sudden testicular pain needs urgent care.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Royal Berkshire NHS (2024) — Pelvic floor strengthening and relaxation exercises for men](https://www.royalberkshire.nhs.uk/media/avwg3hls/pelvic-floor-strengthening-and-relaxation-exercises-for-men_jun24.pdf) — Physiotherapy patient guidance. Supports: Strengthening and relaxation; not a prescription to tighten for everyone.\n\n2. [NIDDK — Symptoms & Causes of Erectile Dysfunction](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes) — Public health guidance. Supports: Vascular, neurological, medication and psychological causes of ED.\n\n3. [NHS — Testicle pain](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/testicle-pain/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Sudden or severe testicular pain requires urgent care.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
