    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nAnxiety, fatigue and pressure to perform can interfere with arousal or erections. Difficulties should not automatically be labelled psychological; medicines, health conditions and relationship factors also deserve attention. [1](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes) [2](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/treatment) [3](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nConsider intimacy without a requirement for intercourse or orgasm. Talk about comfort, feelings and the option to stop. Persistent anxiety can be discussed with a qualified therapist.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nLow HRV does not diagnose performance anxiety or cortisol toxicity. Relaxation may help but cannot resolve every cause of erectile dysfunction.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek care for recurrent erection difficulties, distressing desire changes or anxiety affecting daily life.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NIDDK — Symptoms & Causes of Erectile Dysfunction](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes) — Public health guidance. Supports: Vascular, neurological, medication and psychological causes of ED.\n\n2. [NIDDK — Treatment for Erectile Dysfunction](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/treatment) — Public health guidance. Supports: Cause-based care, lifestyle changes and professional treatment.\n\n3. [Devon Sexual Health (NHS) — Relationships and intimacy](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) — Sexual health service guidance. Supports: Consent, intimacy and masturbation.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
