    "description": "Irregular timing can relate to age, contraception, pregnancy, stress, weight change or illness.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nIrregular timing can relate to age, contraception, pregnancy, stress, weight change or illness. It does not automatically prove anovulation, and an inaccurate app prediction does not establish a disorder. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/) [3](https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nRecord dates, bleeding duration, symptoms and medicines. Test for pregnancy when relevant. Bring the record to an appointment so testing can match the situation.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nPopulation cycle ranges are not stand-alone diagnostic cut-offs. Wearables cannot distinguish every cause of delayed bleeding.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek care for three missed periods, persistent changes or intermenstrual bleeding. Significant pain, faintness or possible pregnancy with bleeding require prompt assessment.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Missed or late periods](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Causes of missed periods and when to seek care.\n\n2. [NHS — Periods](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Cycles, symptoms and abnormal bleeding.\n\n3. [NHS — Doing a pregnancy test](https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Pregnancy test timing and early negative results.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
