    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nEstrogen and progesterone contribute to reproductive cycles and other body functions. Levels vary across life stages and cycles. Fatigue or a poor night does not identify which hormone is high or low. [1](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hormones-and-endocrine-function/reproductive-hormones) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/) [3](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/menopause-and-perimenopause/symptoms/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nTrack the symptoms you want to improve, cycle dates, medicines and daily-life impact. Ask whether testing is needed and when to take it rather than self-treating from a theoretical hormone chart.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nTextbook curves are simplified models, not your test results. HRV, pulse and skin temperature do not directly measure estrogen or progesterone.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPersistent cycle changes, substantial symptoms or suspected early menopause warrant professional advice.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Endocrine Society — Reproductive Hormones](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hormones-and-endocrine-function/reproductive-hormones) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Reproductive hormone roles and appropriate testing.\n\n2. [NHS — Periods](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Cycles, symptoms and abnormal bleeding.\n\n3. [NHS — Symptoms of menopause and perimenopause](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/menopause-and-perimenopause/symptoms/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Perimenopause and menopause symptoms; seeking care.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
