    "description": "Some people notice poor sleep, fatigue or concentration before a period, but experiences vary.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nSome people notice poor sleep, fatigue or concentration before a period, but experiences vary. Pain, sleep and stress also affect thinking. There is no fixed weekly intelligence or productivity profile. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nFor two cycles, track concentration, sleep and symptoms alongside dates. Adjust breaks or scheduling if a personal pattern emerges, without restricting yourself according to a predicted phase.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nA cycle association does not establish hormones as the only cause. A diary supports discussion rather than diagnosing endocrine or psychiatric illness.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek support when mood or concentration problems impair work or relationships, or persist throughout the month.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — PMS (premenstrual syndrome)](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/) — Public health guidance. Supports: PMS symptoms, symptom diaries and care options.\n\n2. [NHS — Periods](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Cycles, symptoms and abnormal bleeding.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
