    "description": "Desire can change with health, medicines, stress, relationships, pain and life stage.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nDesire can change with health, medicines, stress, relationships, pain and life stage. Lower desire does not by itself establish hormone deficiency. Wanting or not wanting sex deserves respect. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) [2](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/when-sex-is-painful) [3](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nIf the change is distressing, note onset, medication and dryness or pain. Discuss comfort and preferences with your partner and seek advice about possible contributors.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThere is no universal desired frequency. Temperature, HRV and predicted ovulation do not determine how much desire you should have.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek care for distressing changes, pain or suspected medication effects. Do not stop prescribed medicine independently.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Low sex drive (loss of libido)](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Multiple influences on desire and cause-based care.\n\n2. [ACOG — When Sex Is Painful](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/when-sex-is-painful) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Causes and care of painful sex.\n\n3. [Devon Sexual Health (NHS) — Relationships and intimacy](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) — Sexual health service guidance. Supports: Consent, intimacy and masturbation.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
