    "description": "PMS can bring irritability or mood changes before bleeding for some people.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nPMS can bring irritability or mood changes before bleeding for some people. Not everyone feels best around ovulation or worse on the same day. Life circumstances remain important. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nTrack mood, sleep and significant events for at least two cycles. Try a practical adjustment such as regular breaks or a lighter schedule during difficult symptoms, and share the diary when seeking advice.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nAn app does not measure serotonin or predict exact good and bad mood days. Do not attribute all psychological symptoms to the cycle.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSevere symptoms, daily-life impairment or symptoms that do not ease after the period need assessment.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — PMS (premenstrual syndrome)](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/) — Public health guidance. Supports: PMS symptoms, symptom diaries and care options.\n\n2. [NHS — Periods](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Cycles, symptoms and abnormal bleeding.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
