    "description": "Discharge can change normally during the cycle.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nDischarge can change normally during the cycle. Odor, itching, pain or a marked change can signal a need for assessment, but color or pH alone cannot identify the cause. [1](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/vulvovaginal-health) [2](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/when-sex-is-painful)\n\n## What you can do\n\nClean the external area gently and avoid douching or irritating products. Note new symptoms and recent products. Seek assessment rather than selecting antibiotics or antifungals yourself.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nHome pH tests and discharge photographs do not replace assessment or appropriate tests. Variation from a textbook microbiome is not automatically disease.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPelvic pain, fever, blood-stained discharge, possible STI exposure or symptoms in pregnancy warrant prompt advice.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [ACOG — Vulvovaginal Health](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/vulvovaginal-health) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Vulvovaginal care, discharge, tissue changes and treatments.\n\n2. [ACOG — When Sex Is Painful](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/when-sex-is-painful) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Causes and care of painful sex.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
