    "description": "Pain can relate to dryness, irritation, infection, pelvic muscles or pelvic conditions.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nPain can relate to dryness, irritation, infection, pelvic muscles or pelvic conditions. Its location and timing help guide assessment but cannot establish a diagnosis. Continuing through pain can worsen the experience. [1](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/when-sex-is-painful) [2](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/vulvovaginal-health)\n\n## What you can do\n\nStop painful activity and communicate with your partner. Consider a suitable lubricant or non-painful activities if desired. Note whether pain is superficial or deep, when it occurs and associated symptoms.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nDo not assume pain means low estrogen or weak muscles requiring Kegels. Lubricant can improve comfort but does not treat every cause.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nFrequent or severe pain needs assessment. Acute pain with fever, bleeding or possible pregnancy requires prompt care.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [ACOG — When Sex Is Painful](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/when-sex-is-painful) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Causes and care of painful sex.\n\n2. [ACOG — Vulvovaginal Health](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/vulvovaginal-health) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Vulvovaginal care, discharge, tissue changes and treatments.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
