    "description": "The pelvic floor supports organs and continence.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nThe pelvic floor supports organs and continence. Leakage with coughing or exercise may benefit from assessment and supervised training, while pain or tension can require a different approach. Not every symptom means weakness. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/urinary-incontinence/treatment/) [2](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/when-sex-is-painful) [3](https://www.cuh.nhs.uk/patient-information/female-pelvic-floor-muscle-exercises/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nSeek a pelvic-health assessment for leakage, heaviness or uncertain technique. During prescribed practice, breathe naturally, avoid abdominal bracing and allow full relaxation.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nContraction strength or repetition counts alone do not establish progress. Kegels are not a universal solution for painful sex or every pelvic disorder.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nIncreasing pain, difficulty urinating, a prolapse sensation or unusual bleeding warrant assessment before increasing exercise.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Urinary incontinence: non-surgical treatment](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/urinary-incontinence/treatment/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Pelvic-floor assessment and supervised training for leakage.\n\n2. [ACOG — When Sex Is Painful](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/when-sex-is-painful) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Causes and care of painful sex.\n\n3. [Cambridge University Hospitals — Female pelvic floor muscle exercises](https://www.cuh.nhs.uk/patient-information/female-pelvic-floor-muscle-exercises/) — Physiotherapy patient guidance. Supports: Full relaxation between pelvic-floor contractions.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
