    "description": "Menstruation is not by itself a reason to avoid activity.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nMenstruation is not by itself a reason to avoid activity. Gentle movement feels helpful for some, while others need rest for pain or fatigue. There is no mandatory period workout or general ban on strength training. [1](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32661839/) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/) [3](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heavy-periods/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nTry shorter or easier activity when symptoms call for it; keep your usual plan if comfortable. Track pain and bleeding, use suitable menstrual products and maintain adequate food and fluids.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nDo not assume a workout treats cramps by increasing uterine blood flow. Predicted cycle phases should not override symptoms.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPain that disrupts everyday activities or heavy bleeding with fatigue or dizziness warrants assessment.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [McNulty et al. (2020) — The Effects of Menstrual Cycle Phase on Exercise Performance in Eumenorrheic Women](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32661839/) — Systematic review and meta-analysis. Supports: Small average effects and low-quality evidence; individualized approach.\n\n2. [NHS — PMS (premenstrual syndrome)](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/) — Public health guidance. Supports: PMS symptoms, symptom diaries and care options.\n\n3. [NHS — Heavy periods](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heavy-periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Recognizing heavy menstrual bleeding and treatment options.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
