    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nStrength training is part of healthy adult activity. Its value comes from appropriate practice and gradual progression rather than targeting a predicted hormone peak or promising metabolic rejuvenation. [1](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) [2](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37033884/) [3](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073)\n\n## What you can do\n\nChoose suitable exercises, learn technique and allow recovery between hard sessions. Beginners can start light and progress with control. Combine strength work with aerobic activity and adequate food.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nCurrent evidence does not require concentrating resistance training in one cycle phase. More weight or a higher Strain score does not guarantee better results.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPersistent pain, repeated injuries or missed periods warrant assessment and program adjustment.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [WHO — Physical activity](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) — Public health guideline summary. Supports: Activity recommendations by population.\n\n2. [Colenso-Semple et al. (2023) — Current evidence shows no influence of women's menstrual cycle phase on acute strength performance or adaptations to resistance exercise training](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37033884/) — Umbrella review. Supports: Insufficient basis for fixed phase-based resistance programming.\n\n3. [Mountjoy et al. (2023) — IOC consensus statement on Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (REDs)](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073) — Consensus statement. Supports: Low energy availability, bone, menstrual health and multidisciplinary assessment.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Choose suitable exercises, learn technique and allow recovery between hard sessions. Beginners can start light and progress with control. Combine strength work with aerobic activity and adequate food.",
