    "title": "REDs and low energy availability",
    "description": "Problematic low energy availability can affect bone, reproductive health and performance.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nProblematic low energy availability can affect bone, reproductive health and performance. REDs can affect different genders and body sizes. Losing periods is not a necessary or healthy part of serious training. [1](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/) [3](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23247672/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nReview food intake and training with a clinician or sports dietitian when fatigue, poor recovery or menstrual changes accompany heavy training. Do not wait for low body weight to seek help.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThere is no universal smartwatch calorie threshold that diagnoses REDs. Both expenditure and intake estimates are uncertain, and assessment is multidimensional.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nMissed periods, suspected bone stress injury, fainting or persistent underperformance need assessment and may require training restriction.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Mountjoy et al. (2023) — IOC consensus statement on Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (REDs)](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073) — Consensus statement. Supports: Low energy availability, bone, menstrual health and multidisciplinary assessment.\n\n2. [NHS — Missed or late periods](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Causes of missed periods and when to seek care.\n\n3. [Meeusen et al. (2013) — Prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of the overtraining syndrome](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23247672/) — Joint consensus statement. Supports: Overtraining, exclusion diagnosis and absence of a single marker.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Review food intake and training with a clinician or sports dietitian when fatigue, poor recovery or menstrual changes accompany heavy training. Do not wait for low body weight to seek help.",
