    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nStress can accompany cycle changes, but pregnancy, weight changes, heavy exercise, medicines and illness can also delay periods. One unusual month does not establish excessive cortisol as the cause. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/) [3](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073)\n\n## What you can do\n\nTest for pregnancy when relevant. Note changes in routine, food intake and training. Support sleep, regular meals and manageable stress relief, and follow up if bleeding does not return.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nPulse and HRV do not measure cortisol or distinguish causes of amenorrhoea. Self-prescribed cortisol products can delay appropriate assessment.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek care for three missed periods, persistent cycle changes or accompanying symptoms such as substantial weight change.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Missed or late periods](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Causes of missed periods and when to seek care.\n\n2. [NHS — Doing a pregnancy test](https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Pregnancy test timing and early negative results.\n\n3. [Mountjoy et al. (2023) — IOC consensus statement on Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (REDs)](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073) — Consensus statement. Supports: Low energy availability, bone, menstrual health and multidisciplinary assessment.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
