    "description": "Adequate, varied food matters more than a rigid phase-based menu.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nAdequate, varied food matters more than a rigid phase-based menu. Hunger or cravings may change before a period, but this does not establish a required macronutrient split for each phase. [1](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/) [3](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073) [4](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heavy-periods/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nBuild balanced meals and add a snack when hungry or training more. Reassess total energy with a professional if dieting brings fatigue, missed periods or declining performance.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nCraving a food does not diagnose a mineral deficiency. Seed cycling and estrogen-detox foods cannot be promised to balance hormones.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nHeavy bleeding with fatigue or persistent difficulty eating adequately warrants assessment rather than a menu change alone.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [WHO — Healthy diet](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet) — Public health guidance. Supports: Dietary variety and food quality.\n\n2. [NHS — PMS (premenstrual syndrome)](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/) — Public health guidance. Supports: PMS symptoms, symptom diaries and care options.\n\n3. [Mountjoy et al. (2023) — IOC consensus statement on Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (REDs)](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073) — Consensus statement. Supports: Low energy availability, bone, menstrual health and multidisciplinary assessment.\n\n4. [NHS — Heavy periods](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heavy-periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Recognizing heavy menstrual bleeding and treatment options.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
