    "description": "Heavy periods can cause iron deficiency or anaemia.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nHeavy periods can cause iron deficiency or anaemia. Fatigue and exertional breathlessness have many causes; wearable readings cannot measure ferritin. Daily-life impact matters alongside bleeding estimates. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heavy-periods/) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/iron-deficiency-anaemia/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nRecord bleeding days, product changes and daily-life impact. Ask about blood tests and the cause of bleeding. If prescribed iron, follow the treatment and follow-up plan.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nSymptoms alone do not diagnose iron deficiency, and there is no universal optimal ferritin target. Treatment depends on results and context.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nVery heavy bleeding with faintness, collapse, chest pain or breathlessness requires urgent care. Persistent heavy bleeding needs assessment.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Heavy periods](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heavy-periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Recognizing heavy menstrual bleeding and treatment options.\n\n2. [NHS — Iron deficiency anaemia](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/iron-deficiency-anaemia/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Anaemia testing and assessed iron replacement.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
