    "description": "Conception is more likely in the days before ovulation, but calendars cannot pinpoint the actual timing reliably.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nConception is more likely in the days before ovulation, but calendars cannot pinpoint the actual timing reliably. Sperm and egg survival are biological ranges, not exact timers for each person. [1](https://integration.asrm.org/globalassets/_asrm/practice-guidance/practice-guidelines/pdf/optimizing_natural_fertility.pdf) [2](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/fertility-awareness-based-methods-of-family-planning)\n\n## What you can do\n\nASRM suggests intercourse every one to two days during the fertile window if comfortable for both partners. Regular intercourse can be preferable to intensive tracking when tracking creates stress.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nA study probability chart is not an individual forecast. Tracking cannot guarantee conception or assess all male and female fertility factors.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek evaluation after 12 months without conception, or six months when the female partner is 35 or older; known conditions or irregular cycles justify earlier advice.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [ASRM (2022) — Optimizing natural fertility: a committee opinion](https://integration.asrm.org/globalassets/_asrm/practice-guidance/practice-guidelines/pdf/optimizing_natural_fertility.pdf) — Committee opinion. Supports: Intercourse timing, prediction limitations and fertility evaluation.\n\n2. [ACOG — Fertility Awareness-Based Methods of Family Planning](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/fertility-awareness-based-methods-of-family-planning) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Mucus, BBT, fertile timing and contraceptive limitations.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
