    "title": "Body changes during pregnancy",
    "description": "Pregnancy changes circulation, digestion and how activity feels.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nPregnancy changes circulation, digestion and how activity feels. Changes vary across people and stages. A rising pulse trend alone cannot establish a healthy pregnancy or show that a symptom is harmless. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/common-health-problems/) [2](https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2020/04/physical-activity-and-exercise-during-pregnancy-and-the-postpartum-period)\n\n## What you can do\n\nKeep antenatal appointments and discuss changes with your care team. Use wearable data as supplementary information and note symptoms, without trying to lower pulse through food restriction or excessive exercise.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nNon-pregnancy reference values do not automatically apply during pregnancy. No single watch threshold guarantees normal pregnancy.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nChest pain, marked breathlessness, fainting or bleeding require prompt assessment rather than waiting for a recovery-score change.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Common health problems in pregnancy](https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/common-health-problems/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Common pregnancy symptoms.\n\n2. [ACOG (2020) — Physical Activity and Exercise During Pregnancy and the Postpartum Period](https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2020/04/physical-activity-and-exercise-during-pregnancy-and-the-postpartum-period) — Committee guidance. Supports: Pregnancy activity, individualization and exercise warning signs.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
