    "title": "Exercise during pregnancy",
    "description": "Many people with uncomplicated pregnancies can benefit from activity.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nMany people with uncomplicated pregnancies can benefit from activity. ACOG describes about 150 minutes of moderate activity weekly, with individual plans based on training history, pregnancy and medical advice. [1](https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2020/04/physical-activity-and-exercise-during-pregnancy-and-the-postpartum-period) [2](https://www.cdc.gov/physicalactivity/basics/measuring/index.html)\n\n## What you can do\n\nDiscuss current activity at antenatal visits. Start gradually when new to exercise and use effort and conversation rather than forcing a pulse target. Avoid overheating and activities with collision or fall risk.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThere is no universal 140-bpm ceiling. General targets do not override restrictions for pregnancy complications.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nStop and contact your care team for bleeding, fluid leakage, regular painful contractions, chest pain, dizziness or breathlessness before exertion.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [ACOG (2020) — Physical Activity and Exercise During Pregnancy and the Postpartum Period](https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2020/04/physical-activity-and-exercise-during-pregnancy-and-the-postpartum-period) — Committee guidance. Supports: Pregnancy activity, individualization and exercise warning signs.\n\n2. [CDC — Measuring Physical Activity Intensity](https://www.cdc.gov/physicalactivity/basics/measuring/index.html) — Public health guidance. Supports: Talk test for moderate and vigorous activity.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
