    "title": "Returning to activity after childbirth",
    "description": "Return to activity depends on delivery, complications and recovery.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nReturn to activity depends on delivery, complications and recovery. Abdominal separation can change over time; width alone does not capture function. Pelvic-floor symptoms also matter when increasing load. [1](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/exercise-after-pregnancy) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/urinary-incontinence/treatment/) [3](https://www.cuh.nhs.uk/patient-information/female-pelvic-floor-muscle-exercises/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nBegin with comfortable light activity, rest and gradual progression. Ask about timing after caesarean delivery or complications. Seek assessment for leakage, pelvic heaviness or pain with movements.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nA six-week date is not automatic clearance for impact or heavy training. No exercise can be promised to close a gap on a fixed schedule.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nIncreasing bleeding, wound pain, fever or worsening pelvic symptoms warrant contact with your care team before progression.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [ACOG — Exercise After Pregnancy](https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/exercise-after-pregnancy) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Postpartum return to activity by delivery and complications.\n\n2. [NHS — Urinary incontinence: non-surgical treatment](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/urinary-incontinence/treatment/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Pelvic-floor assessment and supervised training for leakage.\n\n3. [Cambridge University Hospitals — Female pelvic floor muscle exercises](https://www.cuh.nhs.uk/patient-information/female-pelvic-floor-muscle-exercises/) — Physiotherapy patient guidance. Supports: Full relaxation between pelvic-floor contractions.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Begin with comfortable light activity, rest and gradual progression. Ask about timing after caesarean delivery or complications. Seek assessment for leakage, pelvic heaviness or pain with movements.",
