    "id": "WH_28",
    "type": "Deep dive",
    "category": "Women's Health",
    "sub_topic": "PCOS/PMOS: diagnosis and comprehensive care",
    "gender": "Female",
    "screen": "Menstrual Cycle & Metabolic Health Screen",
    "metric": "PCOS/PMOS: diagnosis and comprehensive care (educational topic; not a measured biomarker)",
    "trigger": "User taps info icon (i) or 'Learn more' on the Menstrual Cycle & Metabolic Health Screen card",
    "title": "PCOS/PMOS: diagnosis and comprehensive care",
    "description": "The condition commonly known as PCOS involves menstrual, androgen and metabolic features, not simply cysts on an ultrasound.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nThe condition commonly known as PCOS involves menstrual, androgen and metabolic features, not simply cysts on an ultrasound. Monash's 2026 update uses PMOS terminology. Diagnosis still requires clinical assessment and exclusion of other causes. [1](https://www.monash.edu/medicine/mchri/pcos/guideline) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/) [3](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet)\n\n## What you can do\n\nBring cycle history, symptoms and priorities such as bleeding control or pregnancy. Care should address metabolic and emotional health, activity, nutrition and appropriate treatment without weight-based blame.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nAdult and adolescent criteria differ. An LH/FSH ratio, insulin value or app prediction is not a stand-alone diagnosis, and no diet cures everyone.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPersistent infrequent periods, new androgen symptoms or difficulty conceiving warrant advice rather than diet changes alone.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Monash University — International evidence-based PCOS / PMOS guideline](https://www.monash.edu/medicine/mchri/pcos/guideline) — International guideline. Supports: Diagnosis and comprehensive care; PMOS terminology update in 2026.\n\n2. [NHS — Missed or late periods](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/missed-or-late-periods/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Causes of missed periods and when to seek care.\n\n3. [WHO — Healthy diet](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet) — Public health guidance. Supports: Dietary variety and food quality.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Bring cycle history, symptoms and priorities such as bleeding control or pregnancy. Care should address metabolic and emotional health, activity, nutrition and appropriate treatment without weight-based blame.",
    "reading_time": "1 min",
    "thumbnail": "images/editorial/WH_28_hero.svg",
    "source_name": "Monash University — International evidence-based PCOS / PMOS guideline",
