    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nPoor nutrition and inadequate energy can affect reproductive health, but no single food guarantees higher testosterone in someone already well nourished. Fatigue or lower desire alone does not diagnose deficiency. [1](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet) [2](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hypogonadism) [3](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073)\n\n## What you can do\n\nMaintain varied meals and enough energy for daily life and training. Reconsider restrictive dieting with professional help. Discuss persistent symptoms before buying testosterone boosters.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nNo single fat percentage or calorie-deficit cut-off guarantees optimal testosterone. Assessment requires symptoms and appropriate laboratory testing.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nDistressing desire changes, erectile difficulties or persistent fatigue warrant assessment. Do not self-prescribe hormones.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [WHO — Healthy diet](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet) — Public health guidance. Supports: Dietary variety and food quality.\n\n2. [Endocrine Society — Hypogonadism in Men](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hypogonadism) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Low testosterone diagnosis and testing limitations.\n\n3. [Mountjoy et al. (2023) — IOC consensus statement on Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (REDs)](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073) — Consensus statement. Supports: Low energy availability, bone, menstrual health and multidisciplinary assessment.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Maintain varied meals and enough energy for daily life and training. Reconsider restrictive dieting with professional help. Discuss persistent symptoms before buying testosterone boosters.",
