    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nA pattern rich in vegetables, legumes, whole grains, fish and unsaturated fats fits healthy-eating principles. Cardiovascular health relates to erections, but a dietary pattern does not replace erectile-dysfunction assessment. [1](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet) [2](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes) [3](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/treatment)\n\n## What you can do\n\nStart with a manageable change: add legumes or vegetables or replace some processed meat with fish or plant protein. Familiar local foods work; imported products and wine are unnecessary.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nDo not treat the pattern as guaranteed disease protection or an erectile-dysfunction cure. Overall lifestyle and medical conditions also matter.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nRecurrent erection problems need assessment, including cardiovascular, metabolic and medication factors when appropriate.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [WHO — Healthy diet](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet) — Public health guidance. Supports: Dietary variety and food quality.\n\n2. [NIDDK — Symptoms & Causes of Erectile Dysfunction](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes) — Public health guidance. Supports: Vascular, neurological, medication and psychological causes of ED.\n\n3. [NIDDK — Treatment for Erectile Dysfunction](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/treatment) — Public health guidance. Supports: Cause-based care, lifestyle changes and professional treatment.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
