    "title": "Protein needs for resistance training",
    "description": "Protein supports adaptation alongside resistance training.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nProtein supports adaptation alongside resistance training. A meta-analysis in healthy adults found diminishing average additional benefits near total intakes of 1.6 g/kg/day. This is a research estimate, not a universal minimum or maximum. [1](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28698222/) [2](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet)\n\n## What you can do\n\nFor illustration, 70 kg at 1.6 g/kg is about 112 g daily. Spread suitable foods such as fish, eggs, dairy or legumes across meals. Check your food intake before buying protein powder.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nMore protein does not indefinitely produce more muscle. Evidence in healthy adults cannot establish safety for every kidney condition.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nKidney disease, nutrition-affecting illness or prescribed protein restriction require an individualized target.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Morton et al. (2018) — A systematic review, meta-analysis and meta-regression of the effect of protein supplementation on resistance training-induced gains in muscle mass and strength in healthy adults](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28698222/) — Systematic review and meta-analysis. Supports: Protein with resistance training in healthy adults; not directly applicable to kidney disease.\n\n2. [WHO — Healthy diet](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet) — Public health guidance. Supports: Dietary variety and food quality.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
