    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nAlcohol can make you sleepy without providing better sleep, and effects vary with intake and context. It also carries wider health risks. A fixed number of drinks cannot be labelled completely safe or beneficial for hormones. [1](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/alcohol) [2](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-deprivation/healthy-sleep-habits)\n\n## What you can do\n\nTry reducing or avoiding alcohol and note sleep, next-day wellbeing and training. Alcohol is not a required recovery aid or healthy-diet component.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nOne night of elevated pulse cannot quantify a testosterone change. Drinks cannot be converted into fixed hormone-loss percentages.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek medical support if controlling intake is difficult or you have experienced withdrawal symptoms.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [WHO — Alcohol](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/alcohol) — Public health evidence summary. Supports: Alcohol-related health risks; no completely risk-free consumption.\n\n2. [NHLBI — Healthy Sleep Habits](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-deprivation/healthy-sleep-habits) — Public health guidance. Supports: Sleep habits and consistent schedules.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
