    "description": "Stress and anxiety can contribute to lower desire or erection difficulties, but they are only part of the picture.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nStress and anxiety can contribute to lower desire or erection difficulties, but they are only part of the picture. Physiological models do not demonstrate harmful cortisol levels in an individual. [1](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes) [2](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) [3](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/treatment)\n\n## What you can do\n\nIdentify pressure-provoking situations and discuss comfortable pacing with your partner. Support sleep, breaks and activity. Include medicines and medical history in advice for persistent symptoms.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nHRV or perceived tension cannot quantify hormone levels. Relaxation may help but does not replace treatment of medical causes.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nRecurrent erection problems, pain or distressing desire changes warrant assessment even during clear stress.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NIDDK — Symptoms & Causes of Erectile Dysfunction](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes) — Public health guidance. Supports: Vascular, neurological, medication and psychological causes of ED.\n\n2. [NHS — Low sex drive (loss of libido)](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Multiple influences on desire and cause-based care.\n\n3. [NIDDK — Treatment for Erectile Dysfunction](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/treatment) — Public health guidance. Supports: Cause-based care, lifestyle changes and professional treatment.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
