    "title": "Building emotional intimacy",
    "description": "Emotional intimacy can grow through listening and respect.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nEmotional intimacy can grow through listening and respect. A hormone cannot measure the bond, and a fixed duration of touch cannot guarantee trust. People differ in how much closeness feels comfortable. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/lifes-challenges/maintaining-healthy-relationships-and-mental-wellbeing/) [2](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nPractice suggestion: ask about something your partner wants to share and listen before advising. Agree on comfortable connection, such as a walk, conversation or shared time without compulsory touch.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThis is relationship practice, not an oxytocin prescription. There is no required amount of disclosure or universal pace of intimacy.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nRepeated conflict or distress may benefit from counselling. Respect the option not to disclose something before readiness.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Maintaining healthy relationships and mental wellbeing](https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/lifes-challenges/maintaining-healthy-relationships-and-mental-wellbeing/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Communication, boundaries and managing disagreements.\n\n2. [Devon Sexual Health (NHS) — Relationships and intimacy](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) — Sexual health service guidance. Supports: Consent, intimacy and masturbation.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
