    "title": "When stress affects a relationship",
    "description": "Life or work pressures can bring irritability or withdrawal and affect interactions.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nLife or work pressures can bring irritability or withdrawal and affect interactions. This does not mean emotions spread like an infection or that matching HRV proves a healthy relationship. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/lifes-challenges/maintaining-healthy-relationships-and-mental-wellbeing/) [2](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39514915/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nPractice suggestion: explain whether you need listening, practical help or quiet time. Agree when to reconnect so a pause does not become abandonment. Both people can set support boundaries.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nDo not reduce conflict to cortisol or the nervous system. Self-help does not replace addressing disrespect or stressful circumstances.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek support when persistent stress affects sleep, work or relationship safety.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Maintaining healthy relationships and mental wellbeing](https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/lifes-challenges/maintaining-healthy-relationships-and-mental-wellbeing/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Communication, boundaries and managing disagreements.\n\n2. [The association between couple relationships and sleep: A systematic review and meta-analysis](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39514915/) — Systematic review and meta-analysis. Supports: Associations between sleep and relationship quality.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Practice suggestion: explain whether you need listening, practical help or quiet time. Agree when to reconnect so a pause does not become abandonment. Both people can set support boundaries.",
