    "description": "Health, medicines, mood, context and relationships all influence desire.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nHealth, medicines, mood, context and relationships all influence desire. Testosterone is one factor, but fluctuating or lower desire does not itself establish deficiency. Men are not required to always want sex. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) [2](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hypogonadism) [3](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nIf changes are distressing, note timing, context and associated symptoms. Talk without blame and consider fatigue, pain, stress and medicines during assessment.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nPulse and HRV cannot score desire or dopamine. There is no universal normal frequency of sex.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek care for distress, associated erection difficulties or suspected medication effects without stopping prescribed treatment independently.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Low sex drive (loss of libido)](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Multiple influences on desire and cause-based care.\n\n2. [Endocrine Society — Hypogonadism in Men](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hypogonadism) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Low testosterone diagnosis and testing limitations.\n\n3. [Devon Sexual Health (NHS) — Relationships and intimacy](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) — Sexual health service guidance. Supports: Consent, intimacy and masturbation.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
