    "title": "Does low desire always mean low testosterone?",
    "description": "Desire can change without testosterone deficiency, while deficiency requires compatible symptoms and appropriate test results.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nDesire can change without testosterone deficiency, while deficiency requires compatible symptoms and appropriate test results. Stress, medication, illness and relationships can contribute together. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) [2](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29562364/) [3](https://integration.asrm.org/practice-guidance/practice-committee-documents/diagnosis-and-treatment-of-infertility-in-men-aua-asrm-guideline-part2/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nIdentify the distressing change and its context. If assessment is appropriate, discuss repeated morning testing and alternative causes. State fertility plans before considering treatment.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nAn in-range testosterone result does not explain all sexual experience. Raising levels without deficiency does not guarantee improved desire or satisfaction.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPersistent symptoms or possible medication effects warrant care. Avoid self-prescribed hormones and boosters.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Low sex drive (loss of libido)](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Multiple influences on desire and cause-based care.\n\n2. [Bhasin et al. (2018) — Testosterone Therapy in Men With Hypogonadism: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29562364/) — Clinical practice guideline. Supports: Symptoms plus repeated morning tests; avoid testosterone when planning fertility.\n\n3. [AUA/ASRM — Diagnosis and treatment of infertility in men: guideline part II](https://integration.asrm.org/practice-guidance/practice-committee-documents/diagnosis-and-treatment-of-infertility-in-men-aua-asrm-guideline-part2/) — Clinical practice guideline. Supports: Limited supplement evidence and testosterone-related fertility concerns.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
