    "title": "Is there a normal amount of sexual desire?",
    "description": "Desired sexual frequency varies widely.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nDesired sexual frequency varies widely. Willingness, comfort and whether a change is distressing matter more than comparison with a number. Low or absent desire is not automatically a disorder. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) [2](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nPractice suggestion: describe your preferences without setting a minimum quota. Agree on comfortable forms of intimacy and the right to stop rather than turning a schedule into an obligation.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nTheoretical excitation and inhibition models do not diagnose individuals. Sexual frequency alone does not measure relationship quality.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek advice for distressing changes, pain, possible medication effects or pressure and coercion.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Low sex drive (loss of libido)](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Multiple influences on desire and cause-based care.\n\n2. [Devon Sexual Health (NHS) — Relationships and intimacy](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) — Sexual health service guidance. Supports: Consent, intimacy and masturbation.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Practice suggestion: describe your preferences without setting a minimum quota. Agree on comfortable forms of intimacy and the right to stop rather than turning a schedule into an obligation.",
