    "description": "Human trials on fasting and reproductive hormones are limited and heterogeneous.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nHuman trials on fasting and reproductive hormones are limited and heterogeneous. Some found lower testosterone without reduced strength. Fasting should not be marketed as a guaranteed testosterone booster. [1](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35684143/) [2](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet) [3](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073)\n\n## What you can do\n\nIf trying it, choose a schedule that permits adequate food, comfortable training and sleep. Monitor hunger, fatigue, performance and sustainability. Seek individual advice with diabetes medicines, illness or an eating-disorder history.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nFasting duration is not the only variable; energy and food composition also differ between studies. A hormone change alone does not establish disease.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nStop and seek advice for faintness, suspected low blood glucose, persistent fatigue or inadequate intake.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Cienfuegos et al. (2022) — Effect of Intermittent Fasting on Reproductive Hormone Levels in Females and Males: A Review of Human Trials](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35684143/) — Review of human trials. Supports: Limited hormone trials; no basis to promise a testosterone increase.\n\n2. [WHO — Healthy diet](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet) — Public health guidance. Supports: Dietary variety and food quality.\n\n3. [Mountjoy et al. (2023) — IOC consensus statement on Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (REDs)](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073) — Consensus statement. Supports: Low energy availability, bone, menstrual health and multidisciplinary assessment.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
