    "description": "Erections during sleep or on waking can occur normally and vary with sleep, medication, age and health.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nErections during sleep or on waking can occur normally and vary with sleep, medication, age and health. Not noticing one every morning does not diagnose vascular disease or low testosterone. [1](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes) [2](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hypogonadism)\n\n## What you can do\n\nConsider whether a persistent change accompanies erection difficulties elsewhere, pain or distressing desire changes. Record medicines and sleep for an appointment rather than repeatedly testing yourself each morning.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nMorning erections do not exclude every cause of ED. Consumer sleep trackers do not measure rigidity or hormone levels.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek assessment for recurrent erection difficulties or distressing changes rather than self-medicating from this sign.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NIDDK — Symptoms & Causes of Erectile Dysfunction](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/symptoms-causes) — Public health guidance. Supports: Vascular, neurological, medication and psychological causes of ED.\n\n2. [Endocrine Society — Hypogonadism in Men](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hypogonadism) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Low testosterone diagnosis and testing limitations.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
