    "title": "Physical activity and erectile health",
    "description": "Activity supports cardiovascular health and can form part of ED care.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nActivity supports cardiovascular health and can form part of ED care. No cardio or pelvic-floor exercise treats every cause. A wearable fitness score does not measure erection quality. [1](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/treatment) [2](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) [3](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073)\n\n## What you can do\n\nChoose sustainable activity and progress gradually. Combine exercise with assessment of medical, medication and psychological factors and treatment options rather than simply adding sessions.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nSexual frequency and temporary post-exercise hormone changes do not establish training benefit. Excessive exercise with inadequate energy can be harmful.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nRecurrent ED warrants assessment. Chest pain or exertional breathlessness should be evaluated before increasing training.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NIDDK — Treatment for Erectile Dysfunction](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/urologic-diseases/erectile-dysfunction/treatment) — Public health guidance. Supports: Cause-based care, lifestyle changes and professional treatment.\n\n2. [WHO — Physical activity](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) — Public health guideline summary. Supports: Activity recommendations by population.\n\n3. [Mountjoy et al. (2023) — IOC consensus statement on Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (REDs)](https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/57/17/1073) — Consensus statement. Supports: Low energy availability, bone, menstrual health and multidisciplinary assessment.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Choose sustainable activity and progress gradually. Combine exercise with assessment of medical, medication and psychological factors and treatment options rather than simply adding sessions.",
