    "title": "Male fertility: health, medicines and assessment",
    "description": "Male fertility has multiple determinants and cannot be judged from visible semen volume or desire.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nMale fertility has multiple determinants and cannot be judged from visible semen volume or desire. Medicines, external hormones and illness can matter. Cooling tricks and supplements do not replace assessment. [1](https://integration.asrm.org/practice-guidance/practice-committee-documents/diagnosis-and-treatment-of-infertility-in-men-aua-asrm-guideline-part2/) [2](https://integration.asrm.org/globalassets/_asrm/practice-guidance/practice-guidelines/pdf/optimizing_natural_fertility.pdf) [3](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/testicle-pain/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nDisclose medicines, testosterone, anabolic steroids and supplements when planning conception. A clinician can arrange and interpret semen testing in the couple's context. Do not stop prescribed treatment independently.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nA fixed wait or avoiding one heat exposure cannot be promised to correct all fertility problems. One semen result cannot guarantee conception.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nTesticular pain or swelling needs assessment. Fertility evaluation depends on duration, age and both partners' risk factors.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [AUA/ASRM — Diagnosis and treatment of infertility in men: guideline part II](https://integration.asrm.org/practice-guidance/practice-committee-documents/diagnosis-and-treatment-of-infertility-in-men-aua-asrm-guideline-part2/) — Clinical practice guideline. Supports: Limited supplement evidence and testosterone-related fertility concerns.\n\n2. [ASRM (2022) — Optimizing natural fertility: a committee opinion](https://integration.asrm.org/globalassets/_asrm/practice-guidance/practice-guidelines/pdf/optimizing_natural_fertility.pdf) — Committee opinion. Supports: Intercourse timing, prediction limitations and fertility evaluation.\n\n3. [NHS — Testicle pain](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/testicle-pain/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Sudden or severe testicular pain requires urgent care.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
