    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nGuidelines do not recommend routine testosterone screening in asymptomatic men. Suspected deficiency requires compatible symptoms, reliable tests and confirmation. Fatigue, weight change or low recovery scores have other explanations. [1](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29562364/) [2](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hypogonadism)\n\n## What you can do\n\nDiscuss symptoms, onset, medicines and recent illness. If testing is indicated, ask about preparation, timing and repeat measurement. State fertility plans before considering treatment.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nOne result or an online reference interval cannot establish diagnosis. Total, free and related hormone tests require contextual interpretation.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPersistent symptoms or abnormal results need clinician review rather than broad self-testing and self-prescribed hormones.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Bhasin et al. (2018) — Testosterone Therapy in Men With Hypogonadism: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29562364/) — Clinical practice guideline. Supports: Symptoms plus repeated morning tests; avoid testosterone when planning fertility.\n\n2. [Endocrine Society — Hypogonadism in Men](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hypogonadism) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Low testosterone diagnosis and testing limitations.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
