    "title": "Managing disagreements with less harm",
    "description": "Disagreement can occur in relationships.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nDisagreement can occur in relationships. Strong emotions can make listening harder, but no universal pulse threshold defines loss of communication ability. Respect remains necessary during conflict. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/lifes-challenges/maintaining-healthy-relationships-and-mental-wellbeing/) [2](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nPractice suggestion: agree a pause signal and return time and address one issue. Describe events and needs rather than attacking character, and check understanding when resuming.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nA pause should not become punitive silence or indefinite avoidance. This is not a treatment formula for every conflict.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nThreats, coercion or violence require safety and individual support rather than argument skills alone.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Maintaining healthy relationships and mental wellbeing](https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/lifes-challenges/maintaining-healthy-relationships-and-mental-wellbeing/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Communication, boundaries and managing disagreements.\n\n2. [Devon Sexual Health (NHS) — Relationships and intimacy](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) — Sexual health service guidance. Supports: Consent, intimacy and masturbation.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
