    "id": "MW_REL_06",
    "type": "Deep dive",
    "category": "Men's Health",
    "sub_topic": "When partners want different amounts of sex",
    "gender": "Male",
    "screen": "Sexual Desire Dynamics & Intimacy Screen",
    "metric": "When partners want different amounts of sex (educational topic; not a measured biomarker)",
    "trigger": "User taps info icon (i) or 'Learn more' on the Sexual Desire Dynamics & Intimacy Screen card",
    "title": "When partners want different amounts of sex",
    "description": "Different levels of desire do not automatically mean illness or loss of love.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nDifferent levels of desire do not automatically mean illness or loss of love. Health, stress, medication and context can affect desire. Nobody owes sex as proof of affection. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) [2](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) [3](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30777780/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nDiscuss comfort, pressure and preferred forms of intimacy at a calm time. Make space for closeness without requiring sex, with either person free to change their mind or stop.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nResponsive desire does not mean someone should start unwanted activity and expect to enjoy it. A psychological model is not an obligation.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPersistent distress may benefit from counselling. Pain, medication effects or sudden changes need medical assessment.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Low sex drive (loss of libido)](https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/loss-of-libido/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Multiple influences on desire and cause-based care.\n\n2. [Devon Sexual Health (NHS) — Relationships and intimacy](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) — Sexual health service guidance. Supports: Consent, intimacy and masturbation.\n\n3. [Mallory et al. (2019) — Couples' Sexual Communication and Dimensions of Sexual Function: A Meta-Analysis](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30777780/) — Meta-analysis of associations. Supports: Association of communication and sexual function; not proof of causation.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Discuss comfort, pressure and preferred forms of intimacy at a calm time. Make space for closeness without requiring sex, with either person free to change their mind or stop.",
