    "title": "Trust through observable actions",
    "description": "Trust relates to respect, consistency and boundaries.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nTrust relates to respect, consistency and boundaries. An oxytocin result or attachment label cannot evaluate an entire relationship. Promises matter through the actions accompanying them. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/lifes-challenges/maintaining-healthy-relationships-and-mental-wellbeing/) [2](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nPractice suggestion: make one clear, feasible commitment and communicate early when it cannot be kept. Discuss safety while respecting privacy rather than demanding control.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThis is practice advice, not a guaranteed trust-repair timeline. Phone checks or physiological monitoring are not required proof of fidelity.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nRepeated control, threats or boundary violations need support and safety assessment rather than additional promises.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Maintaining healthy relationships and mental wellbeing](https://www.nhs.uk/every-mind-matters/lifes-challenges/maintaining-healthy-relationships-and-mental-wellbeing/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Communication, boundaries and managing disagreements.\n\n2. [Devon Sexual Health (NHS) — Relationships and intimacy](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) — Sexual health service guidance. Supports: Consent, intimacy and masturbation.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
