    "description": "Orgasm is a sensory experience and ejaculation expels semen.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nOrgasm is a sensory experience and ejaculation expels semen. They often coincide but can occur separately. Medicines, illness and surgery can alter them, and recovery time varies between people and encounters. [1](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/ejaculation-problems/) [2](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/)\n\n## What you can do\n\nRecord timing, pain and medicines when changes are distressing. Discuss expectations without requiring a particular response, and seek advice for persistent difficulties.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThere is no universal refractory period or guaranteed shortcut. Semen volume does not measure orgasm quality or testosterone.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nPainful ejaculation, a new inability to ejaculate, blood in semen or persistent difficulties warrant assessment.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [NHS — Ejaculation problems](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/ejaculation-problems/) — Public health guidance. Supports: Ejaculatory difficulties and treatment options.\n\n2. [Devon Sexual Health (NHS) — Relationships and intimacy](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) — Sexual health service guidance. Supports: Consent, intimacy and masturbation.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
