    "title": "Masturbation and testosterone myths",
    "description": "Masturbation can be a normal voluntary sexual behavior.",
    "content": "## Understanding the topic\n\nMasturbation can be a normal voluntary sexual behavior. Short-term hormone observations from small studies do not establish that abstinence sustainably boosts testosterone or that masturbation depletes it. [1](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) [2](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hypogonadism)\n\n## What you can do\n\nChoose a comfortable frequency, maintain hygiene and avoid painful friction. Seek support if it disrupts sleep, work or a sense of control rather than judging yourself by a count.\n\n## What the evidence cannot tell you\n\nThere is no required abstinence schedule for masculinity, muscle or normal testosterone. Sexual-behavior advice does not replace assessment of possible deficiency.\n\n## When to seek advice\n\nSeek advice for pain, injury, distress or persistent sexual changes rather than taking hormones to compensate.\n\n## Sources and what they support\n\n1. [Devon Sexual Health (NHS) — Relationships and intimacy](https://www.devonsexualhealth.nhs.uk/your-sexual-health/relationships-and-intimacy/) — Sexual health service guidance. Supports: Consent, intimacy and masturbation.\n\n2. [Endocrine Society — Hypogonadism in Men](https://www.endocrine.org/patient-engagement/endocrine-library/hypogonadism) — Professional patient guidance. Supports: Low testosterone diagnosis and testing limitations.\n\n> Content and sources updated: 2026-10-26. Educational content; clinical sign-off has not been recorded.\n",
    "takeaway": "Choose a comfortable frequency, maintain hygiene and avoid painful friction. Seek support if it disrupts sleep, work or a sense of control rather than judging yourself by a count.",
